    pharm: "1. Emergency — start empiric antibiotics within 1 hour of presentation: IV Piperacillin/Tazobactam 4.5g Q6H (anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam). 2. If penicillin-allergic: IV Meropenem 1g Q8H or IV Cefepime 2g Q8H. 3. If vancomycin indicated (catheter-related infection, MRSA colonisation, or haemodynamic instability): add IV Vancomycin 15-20mg/kg Q12H (dose to renal function). 4. G-CSF (Filgrastim 300-480mcg SC daily) until ANC >500 (consider if pneumonia, sepsis, or expected prolonged neutropenia). 5. Fluconazole 400mg daily if persistent fever after 4-7 days of antibiotics (empiric antifungal).",
    nonPharm: "Strict neutropenic precautions: private room, hand hygiene, avoid fresh flowers/fruit, cooked diet only. Mouth care: Chlorhexidine mouthwash QID for mucositis.",
    carePlan: "Admit to isolation room. Start IV Piperacillin/Tazobactam immediately. Blood cultures before antibiotics (do NOT delay antibiotics for cultures). Monitor vitals, temperature, ANC daily. Continue antibiotics until ANC >500 and afebrile for 48 hours. If ANC remains <100 after 7 days and persistent fever, add empiric antifungal.",
    dtps: "1. Life-threatening febrile neutropenia requiring immediate empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics. 2. Chemotherapy-induced severe neutropenia requiring G-CSF support.",
    monitoring: "Vitals q4h. Temperature q4h. Daily: FBC (ANC), CRP, creatinine, blood cultures if new fever. Monitor for septic shock. Chest X-ray if cough/hypoxia. Monitor for antibiotic side effects. Daily mouth inspection.",
    counselling: "Fever after chemotherapy is a medical emergency — you did the right thing coming in immediately. You need IV antibiotics and close monitoring until your white blood cell count recovers (usually 3-5 days). You are in isolation to protect you from infection. Hand washing is the most important thing we all can do.",
    followUp: "Continue antibiotics until ANC >500. Discharge when afebrile for 48 hours and ANC >500. Next chemotherapy cycle: consider G-CSF primary prophylaxis.",
    pearls: "Febrile neutropenia: ANC <500 and a single temperature >38.3°C or temperature >38°C for >1 hour. This is an oncologic emergency — antibiotics must be given within 1 hour of presentation. MASCC score helps risk-stratify: Low risk (>21) can be managed with oral antibiotics as outpatient. High risk (<15) requires inpatient IV antibiotics. The most common source of infection is the patient's own GI flora (translocation through damaged mucosa). G-CSF reduces duration of neutropenia but does not reduce mortality.",
    references: ["IDSA Guidelines: Febrile Neutropenia", "NCCN Guidelines: Myeloid Growth Factors", "Kenya Cancer Treatment Guidelines"],
